#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b8bdbb-40f0-5e9c-9af6-75ad034239f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{116.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269082} {:answer/id #uuid "8c46e53c-d1ff-5dc9-ad5b-7d8ca4d94a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{111.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269083} {:answer/id #uuid "23bbb5d2-e5e3-5cee-9ce1-e977f27b0c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{122.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269084} {:answer/id #uuid "047be692-7ec1-5921-9e3e-a096d5ab0960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{113.8}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269085} {:answer/id #uuid "00c34ec0-c0f2-5eaa-bd8f-92c1fa6ac6de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120.9}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28b8bdbb-40f0-5e9c-9af6-75ad034239f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{116.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269082} :answer-field/id #uuid "f467919b-d9c3-556d-b9c6-eb60fabcc3ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "852d0fcb-212d-5f7f-bb3f-afcaa190e7c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-101514" :question/problem "Given that $a\\cdot b=-10$, find the angle between $a=⟨-4t,3t⟩$ and $b=⟨2,1⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, since $a\\cdot b=-10$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-10 \\\\ (-4t)\\cdot 2+3t\\cdot 1 & =-10 \\\\ -8t+3t & =-10 \\\\ -5t & =-10 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨-4(2),\\,3(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $a$ and $b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{6}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{10\\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})≈{116.6}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "853ea703-1d96-539c-87e3-e34d3d095438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269138} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f223ca-0fea-5bae-8cdd-b11ad4571b15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{27}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269139} {:answer/id #uuid "12fb3ba3-823c-5cfc-b083-6ce78c505fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{88}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269140} {:answer/id #uuid "e05e7500-7274-5b9b-90bd-0b84dc643510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269141} {:answer/id #uuid "9a09a8bd-4160-5cba-b8e6-8131c8959abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{57}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e05e7500-7274-5b9b-90bd-0b84dc643510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269141} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4556650-d240-5520-9d69-8f8ef1163437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e553e89a-b69b-5299-b9b3-9573916538d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-69831" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(2,1,2)$, $Q(1,3,2)$, and $R(4,5,-2).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨2,1,2⟩,\\,q=⟨1,3,2⟩,\\,r=⟨4,5,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964292/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,3,2⟩-⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨4,5,-2⟩-⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4+0\\cdot (-4)}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5})≈{63}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73d308c4-816e-53bb-9c5e-3a09bfaddfb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269145} {:answer/id #uuid "72673e86-c085-5291-8bec-166da72a0e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269146} {:answer/id #uuid "32c95188-10a9-5114-99b0-cb90db5d77b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269147} {:answer/id #uuid "84cd7a4c-ec32-59ce-a7d2-70ca0fd02759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269148} {:answer/id #uuid "d2487dac-0617-58ae-8a51-f5bdcd054680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73d308c4-816e-53bb-9c5e-3a09bfaddfb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269145} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f26f37a-46b8-5fce-81b3-84be685ab68e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09283c16-ce85-51a9-9e26-ee1e56005470" :question/math-academy-id "q-77159" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(2,4,1),$ $Q(3,4,2)$ and $R(3,3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P,$ $Q$ and $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨2,4,1⟩,\\,q=⟨3,4,2⟩,\\,r=⟨3,3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964292/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨2,4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨3,3,1⟩-⟨2,4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$"}]}
